<header><p class="st-kicker">kingdom · Italy</p><h1 class="st-title">Kingdom of Etruria</h1><p class="st-meta">1801 CE – 1807 CE · capital Florence</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">A Napoleonic kingdom created in Tuscany for a Spanish Bourbon prince in 1801, abolished six years later.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>By agreements between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a> in 1800–1801, including the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-aranjuez-1801">Treaty of Aranjuez</a>, the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/grand-duchy-of-tuscany">Grand Duchy of Tuscany</a> was taken from its Habsburg-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lorraine">Lorraine</a> ruler and turned into the Kingdom of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/etruria">Etruria</a> for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/louis-i-of-etruria">Louis of Bourbon-Parma</a>, in exchange for the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/ile-bourbon">Bourbon</a> claim to the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/duchy-of-parma-and-piacenza">Duchy of Parma and Piacenza</a>.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>Louis I, in poor health, ruled from <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/duchy-of-florence">Florence</a> under strong French influence; the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/state-of-the-presidi">State of the Presidi</a> was added. After his death in 1803 his widow <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maria-luisa-duchess-of-lucca">Maria Luisa of Spain</a> governed as regent for their young son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charles-ii-duke-of-parma">Charles Louis</a>. The court was conservative and pious, and the kingdom paid heavily to support French troops.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>In 1807 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/napoleon">Napoleon</a> dissolved the kingdom under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-fontainebleau-1807">Treaty of Fontainebleau</a> and annexed <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/grand-duchy-of-tuscany">Tuscany</a> to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> in 1808. In 1809 he made his sister <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/elisa-bonaparte">Elisa</a> grand duchess. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maria-luisa-duchess-of-lucca">Maria Luisa</a> later received the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/duchy-of-lucca">Duchy of Lucca</a>.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/kingdom-of-etruria.jpg" alt="Kingdom of Etruria" width="320" height="213"><figcaption>Image: This vector image includes elements that have been taken or adapted from this file · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Etrurian_Kingdom_and_War_Flag_with_Great_Royal_Coat_of_Arms.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1801 CE – 1807 CE</dd><dt>Century</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>kingdom</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>bureaucratic</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Florence</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Louis I of Etruria, Maria Luisa of Spain, Charles Louis of Bourbon-Parma</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Catholic Christianity</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Italian</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/italy">Italy</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Regno d Etruria</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1804&amp;focus=kingdom-of-etruria" aria-label="Kingdom of Etruria on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4950 627 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Kingdom of Etruria lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M5270 883L5270 883M5270 883L5272 925M5270 883L5054 728M5270 883L5054 728"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M5270 883h0M5272 925h0M5054 728h0M5054 728h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5270 883h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5270 883h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Florence. <a href="/atlas?year=1804&amp;focus=kingdom-of-etruria">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
